      "source": "Chegg",
      "question": "Craigmont uses the allowance method to account for uncollectible accounts. Its year-end unadjusted trial balance shows Accounts Receivable of $104,500, allowance for doubtful accounts of $665 (credit) and sales of $925,000. If uncollectible accounts are estimated to be 4% of accounts receivable, what is the amount of the bad debts expense adjusting entry?",
      "answer": "Solution :",
      "votes": 11
    },
    {
      "link": "https://quizlet.com/557237444/acct-301-practice-for-exam-78-flash-cards/",
      "source": "Quizlet",
      "question": "Craigmont uses the allowance method to account for uncollectible accounts. Its year-end unadjusted trial balance shows Accounts Receivable of $154,500, allowance for doubtful accounts of $1,165 (credit) and sales of $1,175,000. If uncollectible accounts are estimated to be 4% of accounts receivable, what is the amount of the bad debts expense adjusting entry?",
      "answer": "$154,500 × 4.00% = $6,180 − $1,165 = $5,015",
      "votes": 0
    },
    {
      "link": "https://www.studocu.com/en-us/messages/question/5702014/craigmont-uses-the-allowance-method-to-account-for-uncollectible-accounts-its-year-end-unadjusted",
      "source": "Studocu",
      "question": "Craigmont uses the allowance method to account for uncollectible accounts. Its year-end unadjusted trial balance shows Accounts Receivable of $104,500, allowance for doubtful accounts of $665 (credit) and sales of $925,000. If uncollectible accounts are estimated to be 4% of accounts receivable, what is the amount of the bad debts expense adjusting entry?",
      "answer": "Answer To calculate the bad debts expense adjusting entry, we first need to estimate the total uncollectible accounts. This is done by multiplying the total accounts receivable by the estimated percentage of uncollectible accounts. Calculation Estimate of Uncollectible Accounts = Total Accounts Receivable * Estimated Percentage of Uncollectible Accounts Bad Debts Expense = Estimate of Uncollectible Accounts - Existing Allowance for Doubtful Accounts Let's plug in the given values: Estimate of Uncollectible Accounts = $104,500 * 4% = $4,180 Bad Debts Expense = $4,180 - $665 = $3,515 So, the amount of the bad debts expense adjusting entry is $3,515. Journal Entry The journal entry for the bad debts expense adjusting entry would be: Account Debit Credit Bad Debts Expense $3,515 Allowance for Doubtful Accounts $3,515 This means that you would debit the Bad Debts Expense account and credit the Allowance for Doubtful Accounts account by $3,515.",
      "votes": 0
    },
    {
      "link": "https://homework.study.com/explanation/craigmont-uses-the-allowance-method-to-account-for-uncollectible-accounts-its-year-end-unadjusted-trial-balance-shows-accounts-receivable-of-148-500-allowance-for-doubtful-accounts-of-1-105-credi.html",
      "source": "Homework.Study.com",
      "question": "Craigmont uses the allowance method to account for uncollectible accounts. Its year-end unadjusted trial balance shows Accounts Receivable of $148,500, allowance for doubtful accounts of $1,105 (credit) and sales of $1,145,000. If uncollectible accounts are estimated to be 0.7% of sales, what is the amount of the bad debts expense adjusting entry? • $8,015 • $9,120 • $6,910 • $8,140 • $8,215",
      "answer": "Uncollectible accounts are estimated to be .7% of sales or .7% x 1,145,000 = $8,015. Therefore, the desired balance in the Allowance for Doubtful Accounts is a $8,015 credit. Since the current balance is a $1,105 credit, the Bad Debt adjusting entry is $8,015 - $1,105 = $6,910 and appears as follows: Account DR CR Bad Debt Expense $6,910 Allowance for Doubtful Accounts $6,910",
      "votes": 0
    }
  ],
